**G’s binary Farey bound survives. Its extension to arbitrary rational curves and its pointwise norm bound do not. C’s height theorem kills M1 as proposed. M2 remains a search problem with a conditional \(L[1/4]\) ledger.**

### X1. Which proofs survive

**Lemma FT is correct for primitive binary points.** There is a shorter proof. In the chart \(|a|\le b\), take a shell \(Q\le b<2Q\). Every admissible slope lies in \(\{t:|F(t,1)|\le X/Q^D\}\), which has at most \(D\) interval components by monotonicity between critical points. In a component of length \(w\), Farey spacing gives at most \(1+4Q^2w\) slopes. The sector over that interval with \(Q/2\le y\le Q\) has area \(3Q^2w/8\) and lies entirely in \(|F|\le X\). The sectors have disjoint interiors across shells. Summing charts gives \(O(A_F X^{2/D}+D\log(2E))\). This closes the primitive binary box error whenever \(D\log E\) fits the relation budget. G’s 667 checks used Bean’s upper bound for area. Thunder’s finite-type theorems and Bombieri–Schmidt’s equation bounds have different hypotheses and conclusions. I found no exact prior statement of FT and would claim no priority for the spacing argument. [Thunder](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0105267) · [Bombieri–Schmidt](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01405092) · [G’s test](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/subthird_g/run_checks.py:413>)

- **K2b.** The factor-count bound \(k\le e\) per norm side survives. The smoothness calculation assumes generic sizes and sufficiently independent factors. Newton identities also give no necessary condition \(k(k+1)/2\le d\) for an individual PTE fibre. The sets \(\{0,4,7,11\}\) and \(\{1,2,9,10\}\) match their first three power sums with \(d=4<6\).
- **K2c.** For a homogeneous degree-\(h\) pullback sharing a degree-\(h\) factor modulo each \(p\mid N\), Sylvester nullity gives \(N^h\mid\operatorname{Res}\) for squarefree \(N\). FT also needs a squarefree pullback. The Veronese map \((u^2,uv,v^2)\) paired with \((1,2s,s^2)\) produces \((u+sv)^2\), whose norm has repeated factors. Tube volume likewise gives no upper bound on exceptional lattice points. The repo already states the valid restricted pullback result. [Corollary C2(d)](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/resbarrier_results.md:212)
- **K2d.** Relative-resultant divisibility survives without a module action. The claimed pointwise floor fails even for *unequal* norms. Over \(\mathbb Q(\sqrt[3]3)\), take \(f=T^2-(N+5)T/2+1\) and \(g=T^2-(N+3)T+2\), for large odd \(N\) admitting the base evaluation. Both link at \(T=2\). Their nonsquare discriminants give quadratic extensions of the cubic field. The candidate consisting of the two roots has absolute norms **1 and 8**, independently of \(N\). RR bounds height; this example supplies only one direction. [Proposition RR](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/grdeg_results.md:133)

### X2. M1 and the remaining counting problem

C’s proof works on reduced pairs. Linear Kneser applies inside each characteristic-zero projection field. Before saturation, a stabilizer of dimension \(h\) gives growth at least \(\max(s,2h)-h\ge s/2\). The product-span and index argument then yields the stated bound, including for non-power generators. At M1’s balance the main term is \(x^{3/4+o(1)}\) and the subtractive error is \(O_k(m\log n)\). Removing coincident sides requires the square-root map and order-index hypotheses of CC. Removing repeated subfields requires tracking fixed scalars, denominators and effective degrees. Neither reduction is unconditional WLOG for extraction. G’s published minimum also admits \(A_2=0\), so it cannot isolate the third generator. [Kneser](https://arxiv.org/abs/1510.01354) · [CC and SUB](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/rankdegen_results.md:190) · [G’s loop](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/subthird_g/run_checks.py:789)

The surviving target is an \(L[\alpha]\)-accessible norm bin \(Y\), with \(B=L[\alpha]\), \(\alpha<1/3\), and \(M\le L[\alpha]\) candidates remaining after unit duplicates and lower-rank relation spans are charged. Under the usual smoothness model it needs \(M\rho(\log Y/\log B)\gtrsim\pi(B)\), with enough fresh rank and surplus rows for fruitful dependencies. A volume excess or a single exceptionally small norm establishes neither requirement. [Exceptional-point ledger](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/rankdegen_results.md:506)

### X3. M2 and M3

**M2** specializes beyond-Johnson smooth-value search to primitive rational slopes on split-prime root lines. At \(\beta=1/4\), \(\log Q\sim x/2\), \(K^2=L[3/4]\), and polynomial-in-\(\pi(B)\) one-witness decoding would preserve exponent \(1/4\). For fixed positive slack G predicts \(L[3/4,2\varepsilon]\) witnesses, already too many to list at that cost. The one-witness primitive remains open. Theorem J covers specified certificates; \(H_{\rm sel}\) is not equivalent to this oracle. Extraction also needs hidden torsion randomness. One repair queries squares of well-mixed secret classes, retaining their roots privately, provided decoding succeeds on that distribution. [JR and \(H_{\rm sel}\)](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/pilist_results.md:45) · [Theorem J](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/sihard_results.md:402) · [Useful torsion](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genusleak_results.md:90)

The squarefree definite-form interface supplies no arbitrary multiplicative coset, so DLB/DLT does not directly transfer. Definiteness alone offers no protection: allowing \(N=p^2\), \(p\equiv1\bmod4\), gives \(\mathrm{Cl}(-4p^2)\cong\mathbb F_p^\times/\{\pm1\}\) by the ring-class exact sequence. G’s claim of trivial genus for every prime input also fails at discriminant \(-20\). No factoring or DL equivalence follows for the stated decoder. [DLT hypotheses](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:473) · [Ring-class sequence](https://gskopp.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/12/cftfororders_kopplagarias.pdf)

**M3** with split multiplier is evaluated O1/O2 product listing. There is an additional counting kill when \(m=B^{\omega(1)}\), \(d\ge2\). For \(N\in[X,2X]\), there are \(O(\sqrt X)\) shifts, \(X^{o(1)}\) products per shift since \(M\asymp\log X/\log m\), and \(X^{o(1)}\) small residues. Each nonzero \(P_A(m)-v=X^{O(1)}\) has \(X^{o(1)}\) divisors. Thus only \(X^{1/2+o(1)}\) moduli are covered. At \(m=B^{O(1)}\), all atoms already fit an \(L[\beta]\) smoothness bound. Finding the products remains the missing operation. [Evaluated O1](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonexact_results.md:78)

### X4. Literature

All eleven arXiv identifiers resolve. Huang’s **v1** computes two logs of a common prime; **v4** is a privacy paper. Zhang–Xuan’s factorial bound exists; v2 lists Zhang alone. The RS results concern prime fields and give no decoder at G’s vanishing slack. [Huang v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08784v1) · [v4](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.08784v4) · [Zhang](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28338) · [Brakensiek et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08005) · [Jeronimo](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.05870)

Tesoro factored 100-bit inputs and simulated to 130 bits. The VQE paper factored **1961**. The p-bit experiments, 213-bit smooth twins, quantum Regev streaming, \(L[1/2]\) forms and square-root-time summation match their narrower descriptions. The refined NFS preprint dates to 2020 and Robinson’s orthotope preprint to 2022. I could not verify a distinct 2025–26 *TNFS-alpha* paper matching G’s attribution. [Tesoro](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16355) · [VQE](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.16632) · [p-bits](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.19390) · [Twins](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17699) · [Regev](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06733) · [Forms](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.03486) · [Summation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.21168) · [NFS](https://arxiv.org/abs/2007.02730) · [Robinson](https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.04999) · [Earlier TNFS-alpha](https://eprint.iacr.org/2019/885)

### X5. Ranking and next experiment

Subjective probabilities for general-\(N\) sub-third factoring: **sparse type-D selection 0.3%, M2 one-witness search 0.2%, evaluated M3 0.05%.**

My preregistered M2 slope decoder found **25 versus 2** smooth witnesses at 80 bits and **12 versus 0** at 96 bits against equal norm-test controls. It failed the work criterion at 80 bits: **554 lattice reductions per hit** versus **474 direct norm tests per hit** over the full boxes. The best next experiment is a held-out comparison against a lattice sieve forcing the **same divisor size**, charging total work per fresh relation. That tests whether steering improves the remaining cofactor’s smoothness beyond ordinary presieving. [Preregistration](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/subthird_c/round2_prereg.txt) · [Exact results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/subthird_c/round2_crt.jsonl)